1.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The line is being treated quantitatively with the normal synalepha/elision bene esse > ben-esse / be-nes-se. The quantities that are now stable across the hexameter majority are:

et L by position before tibi, ti S, bi S;
ben L closed by elision / be L by position in the synalepha, es L by position in esse, se S;
so L by nature, li S, quom L by position before sibi;
si S, bi S, sit L by position before male;
ma L by nature, le S anceps.

Syllable by syllable on the elided surface: et L ti S bi S ben L es L se S so L li S quom L si S bi S sit L ma L le S.

The dominant reading in the discussion is dactylic hexameter. Two notations coexist:

* Textbook normalisation on the un-elided spelling: et ti bi | be ne es | se so li | quom si bi | sit ma le = L S S | L S S | L S S | L S S | L L S, i.e. L S S L S S L S S L S S L L S.
* Elided moraic grouping: et ti bi | ben es se | so li quom | si bi sit | ma le = L S S | L L S | L S L | S S L | L S, i.e. L S S L L S L S L S S L L S.

Colleagues 1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9 converge on this hexameter description, with the first foot et ti bi L S S, quom long by position, sit long by position, so and ma long by nature, and the final anceps of the sixth foot.

Disagreement persists with Colleagues 7 and 10. They argue for an iambic senarius reading, claiming the elision should be read be-nes-se not ben-es-se, giving second foot be-nes = S L iambus, and a scansion LSS SL SL LL SSL SL, i.e. et tibi LSS, be-nes SL, se-so SL, li-quom LL, si-bi-sit SSL, ma-le SL with the final syllable anceps. They contend the hexameter readings are inconsistent on the second foot and over-force the line into dactyls.

Unresolved presentation issues: whether to show the second foot as be ne es L S S on the surface spelling or ben es se L L S after synalepha, and how to package feet 5-6 as L L S vs L S, and the exact foot boundaries to print with spaces. The hexameter with elision remains the majority view; the iambic senarius is a coherent minority alternative centred on the be-nes vs ben-es interpretation of the elision and on marking male final anceps.

